  "summary": "Round 3's inline validation proposal sounds elegant but has a critical blind spot: self-review is inherently weaker than external review. The spec-writing agents (spec_impl and spec_test) operate from the same approved plan and the same LLM reasoning patterns — asking them to validate their own output is like asking the author to proofread their own essay. Systematic blind spots (e.g., misinterpreting an ambiguous plan requirement, missing an implicit dependency in the codebase) will persist through self-validation because the same reasoning that produced the error will evaluate it. The existing review_impl step works precisely because it uses a different agent with a different role and different prompt framing. Additionally, Round 3 underestimates the actual cost gap: looking at the real pipeline code, spec-writing already makes two sequential headless agent calls (spec_impl then spec_test), and adding a third validation call within the same phase doesn't meaningfully reduce latency compared to a separate lightweight stage — the pipeline already serializes these calls. A more honest assessment is that the real question isn't 'separate stage vs inline check' but rather 'is spec quality actually a bottleneck worth addressing at all?' The pipeline's retry loop (up to 5 implementation attempts with fix_feedback) already functions as an empirical spec quality check. Before adding any validation, the team should examine pipeline logs to determine how often implementation failures trace back to spec-level problems versus pure implementation bugs — if specs rarely cause rework, this entire discussion is solving a theoretical problem."
